;
; "The evil Hannibals from Mars", which is a demo and behaves like one.
;
; Its own disk starts it with three lines and no icon:
;
;     type s:text
;     add21k
;     hannidemo2.EXE
;
; The same three, from the drawer the card carries. `s:` there means the
; demo's own s/, not ours, so the paths are spelled out.
;
; Why this one. It opens dos.library and nothing else -- the only absolute
; chipset references in the binary are INTENA and DMACON, which is how a
; program says "the operating system is off now, I have the hardware". So it
; needs no display driver from us: it programs Denise itself. Nothing to
; supply, nothing to arbitrate.
;
; AROS is not expected to survive this, and that is the point rather than a
; fault. What the serial line keeps saying afterwards is Bellatrix's own --
; the chipset core, the census, the interrupt counters -- because platform
; interrupts are not Paula's and the ARM side is untouched by a guest turning
; the Amiga off.
;
CD SYS:Hannibals
Echo >DEBUG: "[hannibals] --- what the demo says for itself"
Type >DEBUG: s/text

;
; add21k is NOT run, and it is the reason the first attempt got nowhere.
;
; It is a 280-byte helper from the 512 KB era: on an A500 the operating system
; keeps the top of chip RAM to itself and this hands it back, which is where
; the name comes from. On this machine it computed an address that exists
; nowhere and asked Exec to free a megabyte at it:
;
;     [MM] Attempt to free 1000064 bytes at 0x3061eea4
;     [MM] The block does not belong to any MemHeader
;     Recoverable Alert! Task: add21k -- Error: 0x0100000F
;
; A recoverable alert holds the task, so the script never reached the demo at
; all. And the machine has two megabytes of chip RAM: there is no top of
; memory to reclaim and nothing this could add.
;
Echo >DEBUG: "[hannibals] --- add21k skipped (2 MB of chip; nothing to reclaim)"

;
; The plane goes up BEFORE the demo, and it has to.
;
; Once the demo takes the machine there is no AROS left to run anything: it
; turns interrupts and DMA off, so no task is ever scheduled again and
; DeniseView could not be started afterwards even by a timer. Telling someone
; to type it first is not an answer either -- this script owns the sequence,
; so it owns this.
;
; What that costs, stated rather than hidden: DeniseView reads the descriptor
; once and installs a plane with that geometry. Right now the aperture is
; 256x256 and empty, so that is what the plane will be built for. If the demo
; programs a different mode the pixels behind the plane are still live -- they
; are republished every frame -- but the window onto them keeps the old size,
; and the picture will be cropped or stretched rather than absent.
;
; The census on either side of this says what the geometry actually was, so
; the next run can be built with the right one. Presenting a frame whose size
; changes under the plane is a real gap in the presenter, and this is the run
; that measures it.
;
Echo >DEBUG: "[hannibals] --- aperture before the demo"
DeniseView >DEBUG: NOWINDOW
Echo >DEBUG: "[hannibals] --- plane up"
Run >NIL: <NIL: DeniseView SHOW

Echo >DEBUG: "[hannibals] --- handing the machine over"
Hannidemo2.EXE
Echo >DEBUG: "[hannibals] --- it gave the machine back"
